@page "/dark-mode"

<PageTitle>Dark mode - FluentCss</PageTitle>

<DocHeader Title="Dark mode and themes"
           Description="A theme is an ancestor class. Build theme-aware styles with the Dark root, or create your own with Theme(name)." />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">The Dark root</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Dark</code> is a shortcut for
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Theme("dark")</code>. It prefixes every utility in the chain
    with <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">theme-dark:</code>.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@DarkExample" />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">How the selector is built</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    A themed utility is scoped by an ancestor that carries the theme class. The generated selector has
    two parts: the theme scope and the escaped utility class.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@DarkCss" Language="css" />

<Callout Kind="CalloutKind.Warning" Title="Theme class goes on an ancestor">
    Because the selector is a descendant combinator, the element that carries
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">theme-dark</code> must be an ancestor of the styled element.
    Put it on <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">&lt;html&gt;</code> or a wrapper element, not on the
    same element that also uses the <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">@("@Dark")</code> utilities.
</Callout>

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">Toggling the theme</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    Toggling is plain DOM work: add or remove the theme class. This documentation site keeps the
    choice in <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">localStorage</code> and applies it before the page
    paints.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@ToggleScript" Language="javascript" />

<Example Code="@ToggleExample">
    <div class="@Css.P(6).RoundedLg().Border().BorderColor(Color.Slate200)
                    @Css.Background(Color.White)
                    @Dark.Background(Color.Slate800).BorderColor(Color.Slate700)">
        <p class="@Css.FontBold().Color(Color.Slate900)
                   @Dark.Color(Color.White)">
            This card follows the theme.
        </p>
        <p class="@Css.TextSm().Color(Color.Slate500)
                   @Dark.Color(Color.Slate400)">
            Use the toggle in the header to switch between light and dark.
        </p>
    </div>
</Example>

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">Custom themes</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    Anything you can pass to <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Theme</code> becomes a theme. The same
    mechanism can drive high-contrast modes, seasonal skins or brand variants.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@CustomTheme" />

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    Continue to the <a href="/colors" data-enhance-nav="false" class="@DocsStyles.Link">color palette</a>.
</p>

@code {
    private const string DarkExample =
        """
        Dark
            .Background(Color.Slate800)
            .Color(Color.Slate100)
            .Hover(h => h.Background(Color.Slate700));

        // classes:
        // theme-dark:bg-slate-800
        // theme-dark:text-slate-100
        // theme-dark:hover:bg-slate-700
        """;

    private const string DarkCss =
        """
        /* .theme-dark is the ancestor, the theme class is escaped */
        .theme-dark .theme-dark\:bg-slate-800 { background-color: oklch(0.279 0.041 260.031) }
        .theme-dark .theme-dark\:text-slate-100 { color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896) }
        """;

    private const string ToggleScript =
        """
        // Apply the saved choice before the page paints
        var saved = localStorage.getItem('fluentcss-theme');
        if (saved === 'dark' || (!saved && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches)) {
            document.documentElement.classList.add('theme-dark');
        }

        // Toggle it somewhere (for example a button)
        function fcToggleTheme() {
            document.documentElement.classList.toggle('theme-dark');
        }
        """;

    private const string ToggleExample =
        """
        <div class="@Css.P(6).RoundedLg().Border().BorderColor(Color.Slate200)
                        @Css.Background(Color.White)
                        @Dark.Background(Color.Slate800).BorderColor(Color.Slate700)">
            This card follows the theme.
        </div>
        """;

    private const string CustomTheme =
        """
        Theme("halloween")
            .Background(Color.Orange500)
            .Color(Color.Black);

        // classes: theme-halloween:bg-orange-500 theme-halloween:text-black
        // add the class "theme-halloween" to an ancestor to activate it
        """;
}
